# cursus/Milestone_0/Libft -- the Common Core's first project: libft.a, a
# library of 43 functions the student writes and keeps for later projects.
#
# HOW THE SUITE IS ORGANISED. The subject turns in ONE thing -- "Program Name
# libft.a" (p.7) -- so the contract has one exercise, "00", turned in at the
# root of the repository: there is no "Turn-in directory" line, and "All
# files must be placed at the root of your repository." (p.7). Each of the 43
# functions is a MEMBER of that library (docs/reference.md, "Suites"):
#
#   c_library(num = "00")   what is the whole library's, once: its Makefile
#                           (ex00_build), the Norm, both compilers, the
#                           prototypes and the t_list layout, the forbidden
#                           layer over the union of the boxes and over each
#                           function's own box (ex00_forbidden_functions), no
#                           global variable (ex00_globals), the symbols
#   c_readme(num = "00")    the README.md chapter V asks for (ex00_readme)
#   c_function(member = "ft_<name>")   one function's own layers: targets
#                           ex00_ft_<name>_*, suite //cursus/Milestone_0/Libft:ft_<name>,
#                           material in tests/ex00/ft_<name>/ (its test_*.c,
#                           expected.txt, clues.tsv), `malloc` held to its own
#                           box's External Functions
#
# tests/ex00/prototype.h holds the 43 signatures (Part 1 from the man pages,
# as p.8 says; Parts 2 and 3 as their boxes print them), and tests/layout/
# the t_list p.14 prints, content first.
#
# The members are in six sections, one per group of functions (the "====
# GROUP" markers), each opening with what its subject pages fix and what they
# leave open.

# THE SUBJECT CONTRACT (tools/subject.bzl), from the header box on p.7 and
# the boxes of each part.
#
# Graded by both: peers at the evaluation, "During the evaluation..." (p.18),
# and Deepthought, "If Deepthought is assigned to grade your work, it will
# occur after your peer-evaluations." (p.3-4). Not a group project.
#
# FILES. "Files to Submit Makefile, libft.h, ft_*.c" (p.7), and "A README.md
# file must be provided at the root of your Git repository." (p.17). ft_*.c
# is a shape that names the files, so it is transcribed as it is, beside the
# Makefile (exercise() refuses only a bare "*.c" there). Strict, the default:
# "Submitting unused files is not allowed." (p.7). README.md's stub in
# deliverable/ holds only an HTML comment (tools/stub_check.sh's md kind), so
# ex00_files is green on a fresh tree and ex00_readme red: the student
# writes it.
#
# FUNCTIONS. Each function's own box: Part 1 (p.8) "must not rely on any
# external functions", except calloc and strdup, "In order to implement the
# two following functions, you will use malloc()"; Parts 2 and 3 (pp.10-16)
# print an "External Function" line per function, transcribed below.
# `allowed` is their union (p.7's "External Function Detailed below").
